peacemachine-dvd-ripper (1.0.21) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Do not start on the NVENC preset when there is no NVIDIA card. NVENC fails
    instantly with "Cannot load libcuda.so.1" and HandBrake exits with error 3,
    which reads as "the app cannot rip" on any Intel/AMD/no-GPU machine. Without
    /dev/nvidia0 the default becomes Fast - H.264 x264, and an NVENC failure now
    says so instead of just "failed".

 -- William James Sova (C0D3D) <william@peacemachinestudios.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 05:07:00 -0500

peacemachine-dvd-ripper (1.0.20) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every UI update now goes through a timeout instead of GLib.idle_add. On the
    Debian test VM idle callbacks were never dispatched, so Find did nothing,
    Start never launched the encoder, and Stop/Remove had no effect - all of
    them queued their work through idle_add.
  * Encodes run HandBrake directly instead of inside `systemd-run --scope`, so
    Stop's killpg actually reaches the encoder.

 -- William James Sova (C0D3D) <william@peacemachinestudios.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 05:02:00 -0500

peacemachine-dvd-ripper (1.0.19) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Start the window from a timeout instead of an idle callback. On the Debian
    test VM GLib idle callbacks were never dispatched, so bootstrap never ran:
    no drive detection, no timers, and Scan disc span for ever with the titles
    already parsed. This is the real cause of both symptoms.

 -- William James Sova (C0D3D) <william@peacemachinestudios.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 04:50:00 -0500

